# Snarfem is a Nim variant with four pebble piles. The walkthrough's hint
# mechanism (flowers drawn in the corners of the screen) is purely
# graphical -- fenshire.zil's DRAW-FLOWERS routine only ever calls SCREEN
# and DISPLAY, with no text fallback -- so it's invisible here. Luckily,
# trying to remove more pebbles than a pile holds triggers a bounds-check
# message ("There are only N pebbles in Pile #M.") that doesn't consume a
# turn, which is enough to read off a pile's exact size for free right
# before actually moving on it. Probing that way turn by turn (pile 1,
# then pile 2, then pile 3, then pile 4, taking 1 pebble for real each
# time to keep the exploration going) revealed this seed's starting deal:
# Pile #1 = 6, Pile #2 = 4, Pile #3 = 3, Pile #4 = 2. That's everything
# needed to play it as ordinary Nim: XOR the pile sizes together, and on
# each turn take from whichever pile brings its size down to (pile XOR
# the running total) -- that always exists when the total isn't already
# zero, and it keeps leaving the jester a losing (XOR = 0) position. The
# jester only ever gets to move from a losing position here, so its move
# is a random flail rather than a real threat; just recompute the safe
# move from whatever it leaves behind. The exact sequence below is this
# seed's specific game: 6^4^3^2 = 3, so Pile #3 (3^3 = 0) goes first.

# [Hit any key to begin playing Snarfem.]

>
3
3

# [Hit any key to continue] after the jester's move. It flails from
# 6/4/0/2 to 1/4/0/2 (takes 5 from Pile #1). XOR=7; take 1 from Pile #2
# (4^7=3) to leave 1/3/0/2 (XOR=0).

>
2
1

# It flails from 1/3/0/2 to 0/3/0/2 (takes 1 from Pile #1). XOR=1; take 1
# from Pile #2 (3^1=2) to leave 0/2/0/2 (XOR=0).

>
2
1

# It flails from 0/2/0/2 to 0/0/0/2 (takes 2 from Pile #2). Only Pile #4
# is left, and it's already the whole XOR -- take both pebbles from it to
# empty every pile with us taking the last one: a win.

>
4
2

>
